-<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><title>Learning to Bake Sourdough - Field Notes</title><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","headline":"Learning to Bake Sourdough","author":{"name":"Eli Warren"},"description":"Lessons from thirty loaves and one patient starter.","publisher":{"name":"Field Notes"},"datePublished":"2026-02-03"}</script></head><body><header class="site-header">Field Notes</header><div class="post-content"><h1>Learning to Bake Sourdough</h1><p>My first loaf was dense enough to serve as a doorstop, but its crust smelled wonderful and convinced me to keep trying. Over the next month I changed only one variable at a time, recording water temperature, room temperature, folding intervals, and the appearance of the starter.</p><p>The useful breakthrough was not a special flour or an expensive oven. It was learning to watch the dough rather than the clock. On cold mornings fermentation took hours longer, while a warm afternoon could make the same dough move surprisingly quickly.</p><blockquote>A recipe is a map, but the dough still tells you where you are.</blockquote><p>Thirty loaves later, I still make uneven bread, yet every loaf now has an open crumb and a crisp crust. The notes matter because they turn each mistake into information for the next attempt, instead of a reason to begin again from memory.</p></div><div class="comments">Six comments</div></body></html>
+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en-GB">
+	<head>
+		<title>Learning to Bake Sourdough - Field Notes</title>
+		<script type="application/ld+json">
+			{
+				"@context": "https://schema.org",
+				"@type": "BlogPosting",
+				"headline": "Learning to Bake Sourdough",
+				"author": { "name": "Eli Warren" },
+				"description": "Lessons from thirty loaves and one patient starter.",
+				"publisher": { "name": "Field Notes" },
+				"datePublished": "2026-02-03"
+			}
+		</script>
+	</head>
+	<body>
+		<header class="site-header">Field Notes</header>
+		<div class="post-content">
+			<h1>Learning to Bake Sourdough</h1>
+			<p>
+				My first loaf was dense enough to serve as a doorstop, but its crust smelled wonderful and convinced me to
+				keep trying. Over the next month I changed only one variable at a time, recording water temperature, room
+				temperature, folding intervals, and the appearance of the starter.
+			</p>
+			<p>
+				The useful breakthrough was not a special flour or an expensive oven. It was learning to watch the dough
+				rather than the clock. On cold mornings fermentation took hours longer, while a warm afternoon could make
+				the same dough move surprisingly quickly.
+			</p>
+			<blockquote>A recipe is a map, but the dough still tells you where you are.</blockquote>
+			<p>
+				Thirty loaves later, I still make uneven bread, yet every loaf now has an open crumb and a crisp crust. The
+				notes matter because they turn each mistake into information for the next attempt, instead of a reason to
+				begin again from memory.
+			</p>
+		</div>
+		<div class="comments">Six comments</div>
+	</body>
+</html>
